  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1045 AM EDT Fri Jul 24 2026\n\nA decaying area of showers is noted over far western Illinois late \nthis morning, slowly drifting east, and NWS Chicago is reporting\nsprinkles. Progress will be limited, however, due to a stout \ndry layer below 18,000 ft courtesy of retreating high pressure \nnow over northeast PA. I did note that some HRRR runs attempt to\nbring light rain to the far southwest forecast area. While \nforecast soundings attempt to saturate in the upper- levels, dry\nair elsewhere in the profile preclude introducing any showers \nto today's forecast. Instead, variable cloudiness today with \nhigh temperatures just a touch warmer than yesterday thanks to \nsoutherly return flow on aformentioned high pressure. \n\nThe primary periods of interest continue to be Sunday night and \nMonday night with the risk of severe thunderstorms, and the hot, \nhumid conditions. A 598dm ridge over the Four Corners amplifies\nby this time, spreading a 50-70 knot 500-mb jet over the Great \nLakes. This, amid a hot, humid environment yields very large \nCAPE values and bulk shear near 40 knots. These items await a \ntrigger for convection in the form of an upper-level vorticity \nmaximum, the incoming upper-level jet, or, more likely, \nlingering MCVs or outflow boundaries from prior convection. \nThese finer details are a bit futile to chase at this distance \nand remain to be seen, so, messaging and uncertainty carry on \nlargely unchanged. Given the anticipated storm mode (squall line\nor MCS), damaging wind gusts would be the primary hazard Sunday\nnight and again Monday night. \n\nBehind these storm chances, upper-level troughing over the eastern \nGreat Lakes nudges the Four Corners ridge westward giving us a break \nfrom heat and storms. However, there are indications the ridge \nbuilds to the east through the week, renewing our local chance of \nstorms.",
